hose receipts by bare check would have failed the pre-arm gate for the entire `_v4` family. THE HARD PART took two rounds: admitting a flagless generator on an AST NAME SCAN (absence of the identifier `preserve_current_frozen_bytes`) proves nothing, because an echo can be written under any other name — the first delta re-audit executed exactly that attack and watched the classifier label a tamper `regenerated`. Fix round 2 made flagless generators DEFAULT-DENIED, admitted only by exact SHA-256 membership in a closed, minimal allowlist of reviewed historical generators whose digests are derived from local Git at the coordinates the ordinal-1 sources name; the name scan may now only REFUSE, never admit. Regression-pinned at both boundaries: the foreign-identifier echo attack is refused, an allowlisted historical generator still authenticates. Acceptance evidence (a) is `what-check-proves.md`; (b) is the non-tautological derivation plus its regressions, verdict A94 MET by the second delta re-audit (`docs/process_traces/2026-08-26-t26-s3/delta2/report.md`, no blocker findings). `_v4` re-verified from post-cure code — default-deny is exactly the shape that could have broken it: all three emitting `_v3` generators classify explicit-flag, projected authoring and anchor+replay pass, custody scripts unchanged, 99/99 committed receipts still validate, +2.310 s median per pack. NOT done here, recorded for the magistrate as follow-ups from the second delta re-audit: D2 (the contract narrates echo-vs-regeneration but shows no literal before/after bytes, and `PACK_DIGEST_DOMAIN` and the pre-authoring invariant are used as criteria before they are taught), D3 (a cleanup error raised BEFORE removal leaves the temporary checkout on disk; both boundaries still govern-refuse, and the committed regression cleans up before raising so it cannot see the leak), D4 (a blobless partial/promisor clone lazy-fetched four missing blobs and returned PASS instead of failing closed — a PRE-EXISTING property of `_historical_pack_tree`, newly discovered rather than newly introduced, and the most consequential of the four), and D6 (the reviewed S-1 manifest pins builder digest `29335e6f…` while the current builder and its sidecar agree on `d72c1560…` — the r4 runsheet generates a current BASE manifest so operation is unaffected, but the cited review record is false, which matters for the imminent mint). Custody: `docs/process_traces/2026-08-26-t26-s3/`. |
| SUPERSESSION-DUP-REFUSAL-01 | P1 Phase Gate | 2026-08-27 | Rule on and then implement write-time refusal in the supersession recorder, which appended silent duplicate records when run more than once for a member and voided campaign membership downstream | Landed as PR #206 (`36dcdd76`). Ruling-first, as the row required. **D-156** was authored and landed by the magistrate BEFORE any implementation, on a ruling packet (`docs/process_traces/2026-08-27-t26/supersession-dup-ruling-packet.md`) built from a Sol xhigh read-only design seat plus lieutenant re-verification of every file:line; a blind Fable refuter then returned SOUND on both lenses over D-156's five premises (`docs/process_traces/2026-08-27-t26/supersession-dup-refuter-fable.md`). Ruled semantics, implemented verbatim: any *recognizable* row (exact `record_type` OR exact `schema_version`) already carrying this `bundle_id` in the **target log** refuses, keyed on `bundle_id` within one log, checked inside the held `campaign.lock` BEFORE the candidate row is constructed and before quarantine bytes are read — and **presence, not validity, occupies the slot**: a recognizable-but-invalid row refuses just as hard, because an invalid competing disposition must never license a second one. Refusal surface follows the `LaunchLineageError` precedent: `SupersessionRecorderError(ValueError)` carrying `.reason_code`, validated against a **sibling** registry `SUPERSESSION_RECORDER_REASON_CODES` (deliberately NOT a widening of `LAUNCH_LINEAGE_REASON_CODES`, so the paper's disjoint-vocabulary claim at `docs/paper/draft-v1.md:742` stays true), wire values `campaign_occurrence_supersession_already_recorded` and `campaign_occurrence_supersession_log_unreadable`, printed through the one existing `error: {reason_code}: {msg}` path at exit 2. The refuter's R-1 residual was real and material: the guard's first draft read `load_campaign_log_rows`, which *tolerates one recognized final tear*, while the consumers read through `supersession_entry_validation_results`, which fails closed to `None` — so a **torn final line that is a partial supersession row** would have been invisible to the guard and fatal to the consumer; FIX-1 drives the refusal from both readers. A later magistrate amendment gave that branch its own truthful sibling code `campaign_occurrence_supersession_log_unreadable`, because refusing as "already recorded" when nothing was recorded misreports the condition and `docs/paper/draft-v1.md:742` stakes a published claim on refusal names being the thing that is true. The fresh read-only delta re-audit (ACCEPT-WITH-FIXES, no blockers) then caught the amendment's own rationale being false in the same way: the message and runbook claimed appending would produce an unusable log, but `append_log` quarantines a recognized torn tail, truncates, and appends (`scripts/run_campaign.py:1405-1416`), so the append would in fact succeed. The truthful objection replaced it — quarantining an operator's torn evidence is a custody decision that must be deliberate and recorded, not a side effect of a supersession write. Six regressions, none redundant: identical-args rerun (API + CLI surface, byte-identical log); **third-occurrence rerun with a different `selected_occurrence`** (the divergence-creating write — whole-window membership would otherwise ACCEPT it while the cooldown join refuses); invalid-existing-row; different-`bundle_id` still allowed (scope boundary); unidentifiable-`bundle_id` log blocked globally (3 subtests); torn recognizable final row. **Defect-shaped proof executed**: guard neutralized → 7 failures across 5 of the 6 methods, only the scope-boundary test passing as designed; guard restored → 6 OK. Suites: `tests.test_run_campaign` Ran 263 OK; `tests.test_analysis_integration` Ran 102 OK (preservation — D-156 ruled NO consumer change and the consumer regressions prove none happened). Also landed: the runbook §11 refusal-table row, the once-per-log rule, the ruled escape hatch (Q1 = A: fresh runs root or non-claim disposition; same-root chained recovery unsupported), and the **mandatory** per-`bundle_id` detection scan — mandatory because the existing D-093 audit compares TOTALS (`raw_count == validated_count`) and therefore reports two *valid* same-bundle rows as clean, blind to this exact defect. NOT done here, deliberately: no consumer change, no historical byte repair (Q2 = A: preserve and refuse), and the two residual rows are DRAFTS only in `docs/process_traces/2026-08-27-t26/supersession-queued-rows-draft.md` — `SUPERSESSION-CHAINED-RECOVERY-01` (Q1-B) and `SUPERSESSION-CROSS-CONSUMER-DIVERGENCE-01` (Q4-B) — awaiting magistrate registration, not registered by this PR. |
| FROZEN-RECEIPT-CONSTANT-STALE-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Stop successor-family PACK_AUTHENTICATION from resting on `CURRENT_FROZEN_RECEIPT_SHA256`, the generator constant that names the PREDECESSOR's freeze receipt | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Landed on PR #214 (branch `fix/packauth-soundness-rows`; gauntlet: design consult → implementation → independent audit → fix round → delta re-audit). The staleness is structural, not an accident: `embedded_generator_bytes()` emits a successor generator by substituting only `CURRENT_FAMILY_SUFFIX`, so every successor family is born one generation behind and `_v4` — emitted from the `_v3` generators — will name the `_v2` freeze-0002 digest too. Executed across all nine packs (`docs/process_traces/2026-08-26-t26-s3/characterization.md`, replayable via `replay-characterization.sh`): the three ordinal-1 packs match their own receipt and pass a bare `--check`; all six successors exit 1 with `generation failed: the current frozen identity requires preserve mode`. ADOPTED RULING: the constant is NOT refreshed per generation and the authentication path stops depending on it. Refreshing is impossible rather than merely undesirable — a successor generator is emitted before its own freeze receipt exists, so the digest it would need does not yet exist, and editing the generator after the mint would change already-frozen pack bytes (D-153 would then force a new family generation). The ruling is recorded normatively in `docs/contracts/receipt_histsem_verifier.md` §'Adopted A93 ruling' (dated 2026-08-26) and pinned by `test_frozen_receipt_constant_variants_do_not_change_the_authentication_verdict`, which holds one derivation fixed, varies the constant through absent / matches-current / names-predecessor / unrelated / computed / duplicated / malformed forms and requires an IDENTICAL authentication verdict with only diagnostic differences. The constant is now read by a TOTAL `ast.parse`/`ast.literal_eval` extractor that never raises and never reaches the verdict; its relation is recorded with `authentication_dependency: false`. An earlier shape that refused a flagless generator merely because the constant existed was caught by the independent audit (F1, blocker) and removed — preserve CAPABILITY, not the constant, now selects the invocation. NOT done here: PACKAUTH-PRESERVE-TAUTOLOGY-01, which shares the branch and stays open on the delta re-audit's D1 blocker. A binding `docs/decision_log.md` entry for this ruling is OWED at the merge gate (magistrate's act). |
| MINT-CHECKOUT-DECLARATION-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Build the mint-time measurement-checkout declaration check that D-154 R-3 re-sites the locality lens onto, so the one real event the absolute pack_root comparison ever caught is caught once, at mint, against a declared checkout | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Merged via #208 (squash SHA to be recorded at merge): `generate_freeze_receipt` takes a REQUIRED keyword-only `measurement_checkout`, and both production CLIs (`scripts/generate_arm_readiness.py freeze`, `scripts/author_arm_readiness_evidence.py`) take a required `--measurement-checkout`; the gate compares the resolved repository owning the pack against the strictly-resolved declaration, fires on the CREATE path only (after idempotent replay returns, so R-3's "once, AT MINT" holds) and before any byte is written (so a refusal spends no freeze ordinal). New reason code `readiness_r1_measurement_checkout` (role `MEASUREMENT_CHECKOUT`, type `CUSTODY`) with its ninth ruled-registry `refusal_vocabulary` entry; load-closure pinned in BOTH directions and the `READINESS_REASON_CODES` census moved 55 → 56 deliberately. All four acceptance items VERIFIED by an independent read-only audit: refuse-outside/mint-inside, two-directional registry closure, a T10 `wtTXN`-class regression across two real git repositories through the real CLI by subprocess (exact reason code, and proof no receipt namespace was created), and supplied-never-inferred (no env var, `window.env`, cwd, `REPO_ROOT`, or pack-derived fallback exists on the declared side). Mutation kills recorded for the gate, both closure directions, and the gate PLACEMENT (moving it above the replay branch goes red). The live `docs/phase_2/window_runbook.md` freeze command was repaired in the same PR; ten dated historical command records were deliberately left unchanged. NOT done here, on the record: the transaction runbook's PROMPT 4–6 text (`docs/process_traces/2026-08-22-t20/real-transaction-runbook.md` is pinned to reviewed head `3c96b18f`, so the delta is REPORTED in the PR body and is the magistrate's to land), and C7's clone-based pre-mint refusal screen, RULED by the magistrate: the sacrificial check declares the THROWAWAY CLONE'S OWN PATH, because the declaration binds the checkout performing the freeze and for that step the performing checkout is the clone. The author-CLI scope expansion (adding the required --measurement-checkout to scripts/author_arm_readiness_evidence.py, whose _parser() carried a prior "--pack-root-only" note) is RATIFIED: that note rules out an INERT step-6 digest flag and cannot forbid a required one, without which the emitted freeze command dies at argparse. Registry blast radius measured twice independently: the entry moves the registry SHA-256 `7a164213…` → `cf6deaae…`, all nine committed freeze receipts already bind registry v1 (`d248fdc5…`) and so add no new casualty, and the magistrate RULED the sequencing: #208 merges BEFORE the `_v4` mint, in the same pre-mint wave as W-10 (#209) and S11 (#213), so the `_v4` receipts mint against the NEW registry SHA `cf6deaae…` and estate 11 covers all three together — no receipt ever refuses on registry mismatch. |
| ARM-PACKROOT-COMPARISON-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Extend the D-154 R-1/R-2 truthfulness cure to the arm side so the arm-side pack comparisons stop reporting a content difference when only the recorded pack location differs | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Merged via #208 (squash SHA to be recorded at merge): the R-1 freeze-replay comparator was generalised into one shared helper, `_pack_mapping_mismatch_kind`, and both arm-side whole-dict equalities now route through it — the dry-run staleness limb and the consumed-arm authentication in the launch-lineage check — with `pack_root` compared under the same successor-scoped rule (generations ≥ `family_publication_first_generation` repository-relative and still COMPARED, `_v3` and earlier absolute per the 2026-08-20 ruling, lexical validation only, no normcase, non-repo paths still refusing). Details are true per branch; no new reason codes. All four acceptance items met, with mutation kills recorded at both named sites. The independent audit REFUTED the first round on a finding the row's own registration missed: a THIRD whole-record `_pack_record` comparison in `_derive_arm_semantics_for_verification` is production-active via `scripts/launch_window.py` → `_verify_arm_receipt` and DOMINATED both cured sites, so the cure was unobservable through the real launcher. Confirmed at the bench, cured in the last code commit of the series, and ADOPTED by the magistrate: a whole-dict site that DOMINATES both cured sites must be cured or the cure is unreachable through the launcher. The fence's "only the two arm-side comparisons" was the registering sweep's CENSUS, not a limit — the PR #192 refuter found two consumers of the recorded absolute pack_root and this row's audit corrected that census to FOUR (freeze identity, dry-run staleness, arm verification, consumed-arm authentication), all now routed through the single shared helper. The delta re-audit's independent census confirms the comparison-site set is now CLOSED at four sites, all through the one helper. NOT proven, by the PR's own record: alias-free relocation end to end (the strict `resolve(strict=True)` guard refuses it earlier by design, so the launcher regression retains the original path as a symlink), live boot-session acquisition, and real family-publication marker replay — the end-to-end test patches `_current_boot_session_id` because the sandbox denies `/usr/sbin/sysctl` and installs a synthetic successor route that bypasses `_gate_family_publication`; neither patch can bypass the pack comparator. |
| CONSUME-CONFIRMATION-SUPPLY-01 | P1 Phase Gate | 2026-08-27 | Give the consume lane a step-6 confirmation supply line so the real transaction's consume-side C-to-S gate can enforce the operator-confirmed step-6 digest | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Merged via #204 (`318f5a70`). The row's goal prose named `scripts/generate_arm_readiness.py`'s `consume` subcommand; that subcommand is RETIRED (unconditional `readiness_usage_invalid` pointing at `scripts/launch_window.py`, pinned by `test_standalone_consume_cli_is_retired_with_launcher_guidance`), so options added there would forward to nothing and would advertise a second consume route that does not exist — the independent audit agreed with leaving it unchanged. The real gap was one lane over: `scripts/launch_window.py` accepted `--expected-confirmation-digest` but had no route for the TABLE PATH, which was only ever auto-resolved to `<custody>/family_publication/d117_step6_confirmation_table_v4.json` inside `_derive_arm_semantics_for_verification` — the same 3e signature PR #188 (`f82247ee`) cured for `freeze`. Cure: `--step6-confirmation-table`, forwarded at all four sites that already forward the digest (`_verify_arm_receipt` in assembly; `_consume_launch_capability` and `verify_consumed_launch` in launch; `verify_consumed_launch` in lifecycle `start`); omitting it passes `None` exactly as today, so every existing refusal and the auto-resolution stay byte-identical. Acceptance: seven regressions in `tests/test_launch_window.py` — legs A-F (valid pair at a deliberately NON-default table location, missing path, missing digest, malformed digest, mismatched bytes, nothing supplied) plus `test_launch_cli_real_chain_reaches_confirmation_authenticator`, which drives the REAL `main()` -> `_verify_arm_receipt` -> `_derive_arm_semantics_for_verification` chain down to a spy at `_authenticate_confirmation_table` and pins both the explicit path and the preserved auto-resolution. Because missing-input and mismatch share the one registered `DEPENDENCY_CHANGED_SET` code, every leg discriminates on the refusal DETAIL text (the F0 lesson from the same sweep); leg E asserts the mismatch detail is present AND both missing-input details are absent. Gauntlet: implement (Sol high) -> lead defect-shape check (reverting only `scripts/launch_window.py` fails all six legs) -> independent audit (Sol xhigh, read-only): acceptance MET, 0 blockers, 2 should-fix -> fix round 1 for R1 (the six legs mocked `_verify_arm_receipt`, so a receiver-chain regression would have passed them) -> delta re-audit CLEAN at every severity. Mutation evidence, lead-reproduced: with the parser and all signatures intact, deleting only the four forwarding kwargs fails the reach test on a recorded-spy PATH MISMATCH, not `SystemExit`/`KeyError`/`TypeError`. Tests: `tests/test_launch_window.py` + `tests/test_arm_readiness_lifecycle.py` + `tests/test_gen_state.py` + `tests/test_docs_freshness.py` -> 126 passed, 4 skipped, 291 subtests. NOT done here: audit finding R2 — `docs/phase_2/window_runbook.md:1207` (E-10 physical launch) and `:1355` (lifecycle `start`) supply neither confirmation argument, and `docs/process/rehearsal-operator-card.md:110` is likewise stale; those are lead-owned procedure edits, reported to the magistrate rather than made under this row's fence. Settle and completion need no pair (only lifecycle `start` replays consumption semantics). |
| MLX-ACID-SIGABRT-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Cure the pre-existing pytest SIGABRT at `joulewise/adapters/mlx_runtime.py:1159` reached via the four ACID tests in `tests/test_arm_readiness_evidence_t0.py`, so the suite is pytest-collectable without deselection | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Landed on PR #203 (branch `fix/ci-reliability-batch`; three rounds: the cure, the audit fix round, and the delta re-audit round that is the branch head). The abort is a nanobind double-registration: `mlx.core` is a C-extension whose native initializer calls `abort()` if it runs twice, and the hidden diagnostic is `Critical nanobind error: refusing to add duplicate key "cpu" to enumeration "mlx.core.DeviceType"!`. It ran twice because `author_environment` injected its synthetic `mlx_lm` with `mock.patch.dict(sys.modules, ...)`, whose exit does `clear(); update(<snapshot taken on entry>)` — `mlx`/`mlx.core` are imported LAZILY inside that context by `_mlx_metal_memory`, so they were absent from the snapshot, were evicted on exit, and the next test's `import_module` re-executed the loaded shared object. Cured code-side on both halves per the row's fence (a process-level abort cannot be contained by a marker): `_resolve_mlx_core()` keeps a reference to the loaded extension, and `author_environment` patches the single `mlx_lm` key. The resolver took THREE audit rounds to get right, and the final rule is the record: `sys.modules` is authoritative while it holds an entry; a `None` entry is Python's blocked-import sentinel and still raises `mlx_core_not_found`; membership in the cache turns on what a module IS (`ExtensionFileLoader`) and not on who imported it — the first shape cached whatever sat in `sys.modules` (test stand-ins would have outlived their own `finally` blocks and fed real evidence), the second cached only self-imports (missing the real extension that `mlx_lm.load()` imports before the snapshot, leaving the abort reachable by another route); and the resolver RESTORES the evicted key rather than stepping around it, because while the key is missing any importer in the process — not just this adapter — re-runs the initializer. Seven defect-shaped regressions, each red-before/green-after: `test_mlx_metal_memory_reuses_cached_core_after_module_eviction`, `test_author_environment_preserves_modules_imported_inside_context`, and the five `MlxCoreResolutionTests` (`..._standin_never_enters_the_extension_cache`, `..._blocked_import_sentinel_is_honoured_over_the_cache`, `..._extension_imported_by_another_component_is_remembered`, `..._resolving_after_an_eviction_repairs_sys_modules`, `..._a_standin_left_in_the_cache_is_discarded_not_served`). Acceptance: pre-fix `pytest -k acid` exit 134 with `Fatal Python error: Aborted`; post-fix, same command with the skips temporarily removed, exit 1 `3 failed, 1 skipped, 32 deselected` and a normal summary; the four ACID skips now read `STRUCTURAL-BLOCKED: fixture R1 schemas require FIXTURE-MODERNIZATION-01 (A84)` (the canonical census prefix — the first attempt wrote `STRUCTURE-BLOCKED:` and broke `tests/test_s0_blocked_enumeration.py` with `AssertionError: 0 != 4`, caught by audit and independently by CI on the pre-fix head; the census moves 17/4 to 21/0) and stay skipped, because this row was NEVER sufficient for them — A84 is; `pytest --collect-only -q` reports 4008 tests collected with zero deselection. NOT done here: A84 itself. RESIDUAL RISK recorded, not fixed: the reference lives in the adapter module global, so an `importlib.reload` of the adapter would reset it; no reloader exists anywhere in `joulewise/`, `scripts/` or `tests/`, and the key-restoration above makes the state self-repairing on the next probe. Custody: `docs/process_traces/2026-08-26-t26-ci-reliability/`. |
| CALEXITS-FOURTH-SHAPE-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Complete the mutation-race terminal-shape taxonomy after run 32739939880 failed `test_forced_auto_maintenance_mutation_reproduces_cleanup_race` with a fourth shape the widened predicate rejects | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Closed on verification via PR #203; the CURE had already landed at `ddb1f633` on 2026-08-24, after this row's text was authored, and the row simply predated it. The preserved attempt-1 log (custodied at `docs/process_traces/2026-08-26-t26-ci-reliability/evidence/calexits-fourth-shape-run32739939880-attempt1-py3.11.txt`, from an archive with sha256 `f60638ebcea81bf5584a722604760a6e617ff6df70c1251222e0e2c486d8108c`) shows `RACE_EXERCISED=0 NO_RACE_PRE_WRITE=0 TRACE_INCOMPLETE=0` — every counter zero, because the test died on its first attempt at `self.assertTrue(pack_evidence.complete, ...)` before classifying anything — and an event tail in which the detached `git maintenance` process enters `loose-objects`, spawns only `git prune-packed --quiet` (exit 0) and exits 0. No `git pack-objects` child was ever spawned: the absent-pack-child shape. The 3.14 job of the same run passed with `RACE_EXERCISED=1`, which is why the shape read as intermittent rather than as a broken predicate. `ddb1f633` covers exactly that — `_classify_pack_cleanup` branches on `evidence.pack_child_absent` FIRST and returns `NO_PACK_CHILD`, the mutation test guards its completeness assertion with `if not pack_evidence.pack_child_absent:`, and a raised cleanup errno still outranks the absent child so it cannot mask a real shape-A race. Each of the five modelled shapes A–E carries a deterministic `_synthetic_pack_topology` regression, and that builder raises `"synthetic topology terminal shapes are mutually exclusive"` if asked for two at once. Stated precisely, because an audit round caught the overclaim: that proves MUTUAL EXCLUSION among the modelled shapes, NOT that the taxonomy is exhaustive — a sixth topology would classify `TRACE_INCOMPLETE` and fail loudly rather than pass silently, which is the property soundness needs. Acceptance evidence: bench, the full `tests/test_calibration_exits.py` module passes `46 passed, 208 subtests passed`; hosted, of the 36 `main` runs whose head descends from both this cure and `a28b55bf`, 31 are green, and none of the 5 failures is this test (four are `test_gen_state` live-ID oracle drift during 2026-08-26/27 bookkeeping; the fifth, run 32813091203, is the identity-pins teardown race cured on the same PR). |
| PLANTEST-RGLOB-RACE-01 | P2 Next Slice | 2026-08-27 | Cure the `checkout_inventory` rglob/scandir race in `tests/test_d117_decode_contrast_plan.py` (run 32745254371: `os.scandir` died mid-walk when a directory vanished during the recursive inventory) | No phase exit-checklist matrix row exists. Closed on verification via PR #203; the CURE had already landed at `a28b55bf` on 2026-08-24, after this row's text was authored. `checkout_inventory` no longer uses `Path.rglob`: it walks with `os.walk(topdown=True, onerror=<re-raise>)` and prunes `.git` and `__pycache__` from `dirnames` before descending, so the git loose-object fan-out directory that vanished under a background repack is never scandir-ed at all. Exactness is deliberately preserved rather than traded for tolerance — a real, non-excluded directory vanishing mid-walk still raises, and the regression says so in as many words. Three deterministic regressions ship with it, driven by `_VanishingDirectoryHarness`, which patches `os.scandir` to pause the walking thread at a chosen directory while a worker thread `rmtree`s it: `test_git_object_store_is_never_walked_and_cannot_toll_the_inventory` (cites the hosted provenance verbatim and asserts the hook never even fires — not firing IS the green result), `test_inventory_raises_when_a_real_directory_vanishes_mid_walk`, and `test_inventory_matches_the_rglob_semantics_it_replaces` (symlinks, dangling links, empty directories). Acceptance evidence: bench, `tests/test_d117_decode_contrast_plan.py` three consecutive runs at `24 passed, 1 skipped, 24 subtests passed` each; hosted, the same 36-run/31-green census recorded on CALEXITS-FOURTH-SHAPE-01 above, in which this race did not recur. Same failure family as the identity-pins teardown race cured on PR #203 and as `EVIDENCE-AUTHOR-GIT-TEARDOWN-01`: a background git process mutating a fixture tree another thread is walking or removing. |
